From e6f35e1c033994197417bb65b5c6de487e4780e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: facetimehd downstream <facetimehd-downstream@localhost>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 09/19] v4l2: fix buffer ownership, tagging and DMA mapping

fthd_buffer_return_handler() matched the returned descriptor by casting its
tag back to a struct h2t_buf_ctx pointer - a kernel address handed to
firmware and dereferenced on the way back, before any state was checked.
Give each submission an opaque generation counter instead, stored in the
slot and echoed in the descriptor, and look it up by value. Tags start at 1,
so a zeroed descriptor cannot match a slot that was never submitted.

Everything else in that path is checked too: a short descriptor, an
unreadable one, a count other than one, an unknown tag and a slot in the
wrong state are each reported (rate-limited) and counted, and four in a row
without an intervening good frame fails the queue rather than spinning on a
firmware that is no longer speaking the protocol.

The slot state, the vb pointer and the descriptor list are now protected by
a dedicated buffer_lock, taken by the interrupt path and every ioctl path
that touches a slot; buffers are collected under it and completed outside
it. Every path that gives up a slot returns its buffer to vb2:
stop_streaming() returns all of them, a failed start returns them as QUEUED,
a failed submit returns that one as ERROR, and buf_prepare() cleans up
whatever it allocated before failing. stop_streaming() no longer waits
indefinitely on firmware that has already failed a command.

The scatterlist walk is rewritten. It used pointer arithmetic over
sgtable->sgl, which is wrong for a chained list, WARN_ON()ed a non-zero
offset in the first segment and then mapped it as if it were zero, and did
not check its page count against the IOVA space. It now walks with
for_each_sg(), records the first segment's page offset, rejects a
scatterlist with an interior gap it cannot represent, and bounds every page
count. The buffer's IOVA base carries that page offset, so an unaligned
first segment is no longer silently misplaced.

Finally, queue_setup() answers as the single-planar VIDEO_CAPTURE queue it
is: it validates the size it is given instead of overwriting the caller's
plane count, and sizes the pool from FTHD_BUFFERS rather than a literal.
